  "avoidBefore": "lower",
  "energy": "Aerobic running on rough ground: elite orienteers hold a mean heart rate of about 167-172 beats a minute in races of 12-100 min, and forest terrain raises the oxygen cost of running by about 26%.",
  "summary": "Orienteering is endurance running over uneven ground while navigating, so ankles, calves and shins take the punishment. The plan uses short, focused strength sessions for single-leg control, calf and ankle capacity, alongside the running that drives the fitness.",
  "evidence": [
    {
      "claim": "In a one-year study of elite orienteers, ankle sprains were 37% of acute injuries, and overuse injuries (shin pain, Achilles, peroneal and IT band) were all in the legs.",
      "source": "Linde 1986, British Journal of Sports Medicine",
      "url": "https://bjsm.bmj.com/content/20/3/125",
      "grade": "moderate"
    },
    {
      "claim": "In elite orienteers who did a 10 min calf, balance and hop programme at least twice a week, the odds of substantial injury were about 75% lower (per-protocol; not significant on intention to treat).",
      "source": "Halvarsson & von Rosen 2019, Physical Therapy in Sport",
      "url": "https://search.pedro.org.au/search-results/record-detail/58730",
      "grade": "moderate"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Running in forest terrain costs about 26% more oxygen than road running, and rough terrain drives ankle injuries.",
      "source": "Creagh & Reilly 1997, Sports Medicine",
      "url": "https://lida.sport-iat.de/twm/Record/4004305",
      "grade": "moderate"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "moderate",
